\corpus ->
    // travel-tier-typed — the chain rule's intermediate facts, computed in the typed arm.
    //
    // RFC-0042's downstream reference case. `travel-tier.sh` beside this file computes the same
    // fixed point in awk over `$YIDAM_GRAPH` and commits the tier itself; this one commits the
    // two facts that account for it — which node beneath the assertion set its tier, and how far
    // down that node is. Two arms over one rule, and the signal names do not collide, because a
    // signal name is corpus-wide and `Signals::load` refuses a name two files declare.
    //
    // The shell arm stays exactly as it is. This is not a replacement: a capability declared
    // `run = { gluon = ... }` is a step a light build declines by name, so the corpus keeps a
    // route to the rule in every build, and the typed arm is the one whose purity is a closed
    // prelude and a typecheck rather than a norm.
    //
    // ── two things about the source layout ───────────────────────────────────
    //
    // The comment you are reading is on line 2 and not line 1 on purpose. `gluon_arm::prelude`
    // is concatenated onto the front of line 1 so that a type error's line number is the
    // script's own; a `//` up there would comment out the prelude's own trailing `in`. For the
    // same reason every line below is indented: line 1 opens the lambda at a column deep inside
    // the prelude, and gluon's layout wants this block further right than that.
    //
    // Nothing below imports anything, and nothing can: a calculator script may invoke no macro,
    // and the import macro is gluon's only route from a script to a native module. Every name
    // below is one `gluon_arm::PRELUDE_MODULES` binds, plus `Some`, `None`, `True`, `False` and
    // `LT` from gluon's own implicit prelude.
    //
    // That refusal is lexical, so it fires on the macro's spelling wherever it appears —
    // including in a comment, which is how this paragraph came to be phrased the long way round.
    // A refusal that parsed first and then looked would be the narrower rule and the weaker one:
    // it would have to agree with gluon's parser about what a macro invocation is, and this one
    // does not need to.

    // ── the claim vocabulary, weakest first ──────────────────────────────────
    //
    // `unmarked` ranks below `open` deliberately, and for the reason `travel-tier.sh` states at
    // length: a node that has not declared its evidence standing has not earned a stronger one,
    // and where the evidence is silent the answer is the floor.
    // Written out rather than inferred. `better` compares two of these, and `<` on a field whose
    // type inference has left open sends gluon looking for an `Ord` instance it cannot pick — the
    // error it reports names `Ord (Array a)` and says nothing about the comparison that caused it.
    type Reading = { tier : Int, witness : String, depth : Int }

    let rank : String -> Int = \tag ->
        if tag == "verified" then 3
        else if tag == "inference" then 2
        else if tag == "open" then 1
        else 0

    // A tag outside the vocabulary is `unmarked` and not an error, which is what the shell arm's
    // `if ($5 in rank)` does. The corpus's own `claim_tag` enum is what refuses a typo; a
    // calculator that also refused it would be a second answer to that question.
    let known tag =
        tag == "unmarked" || tag == "open" || tag == "inference" || tag == "verified"

    let declared node =
        foldable.foldl
            (\acc p ->
                if p.key == "claim_tag" then
                    match p.value with
                    | Text s -> if known s then s else acc
                    | _ -> acc
                else acc)
            "unmarked"
            node.properties

    // ── the supporting chain ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // `instance-of` points at the class definition, which carries no claim and is beneath
    // nothing. Every other outgoing link is part of the chain if it lands on a node here, and
    // `Link.resolved` is what says whether it does: the projection zips each written `target:`
    // with the answer `corpus::Edges` already computed, so this script resolves no path. That is
    // #1080's argument, in the arm it was made for.
    let chain node =
        foldable.foldl
            (\es link ->
                if link.relationship == "instance-of" then es
                else
                    match link.resolved with
                    | Some t -> array.append es [{ from = node.id, to = t }]
                    | None -> es)
            []
            node.links

    let edges = foldable.foldl (\acc node -> array.append acc (chain node)) [] corpus.nodes

    // Counted rather than dropped in silence, for the reason the shell arm gives: a chain rule
    // that quietly ignored part of a node's outgoing links would compute a tier that travels
    // further than it should, which is the one direction this vocabulary exists to prevent.
    let links_to_non_nodes =
        foldable.foldl
            (\acc node ->
                acc
                    + foldable.foldl
                        (\k link ->
                            if link.relationship == "instance-of" then k
                            else
                                match link.resolved with
                                | Some _ -> k
                                | None -> k + 1)
                        0
                        node.links)
            0
            corpus.nodes

    // ── the fixed point ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Each node carries the rank it may travel at, the node beneath it that set that rank, and
    // how many hops down that node is. A node starts as its own witness at depth 0, so a node
    // nothing downgrades reports itself — which is the truthful reading of *which node set this
    // tier* and not a missing value.
    let start =
        foldable.foldl
            (\m node ->
                let here : Reading =
                    { tier = rank (declared node), witness = node.id, depth = 0 }
                map.insert node.id here m)
            map.empty
            corpus.nodes

    // A strict lexicographic improvement on (tier, depth, witness). The tier is the rule; the
    // other two are the tie-break that makes the answer a property of the corpus rather than of
    // the order the edges arrived in. Strictness is also why this terminates: the measure falls
    // on every accepted step and the space is finite.
    // Annotated, and the annotation is load-bearing. Left to inference this is a function over
    // any record with those three fields, and `<` on a field of unknown type sends gluon looking
    // for an `Ord` instance it cannot pick — the error it reports is about `Ord (Array a)` and
    // says nothing about the comparison. A calculator's own reading is a good place for a written
    // type anyway.
    let better : Reading -> Reading -> Bool = \cand cur ->
        if cand.tier < cur.tier then True
        else if cand.tier > cur.tier then False
        else if cand.depth < cur.depth then True
        else if cand.depth > cur.depth then False
        // `cmp.(<)` and not `string.ord.compare ... == LT`: the prelude binds the `cmp` module but
        // not `Ordering`'s constructors, so `LT` is undefined in a pattern here. The operator
        // resolves `[Ord String]` from the same place `map.find` does and reads better anyway.
        else cmp.(<) cand.witness cur.witness

    let relax state =
        foldable.foldl
            (\acc edge ->
                match map.find edge.to acc.state with
                | None -> acc
                | Some beneath ->
                    match map.find edge.from acc.state with
                    | None -> acc
                    | Some here ->
                        let cand : Reading =
                            { tier = beneath.tier,
                              witness = beneath.witness,
                              depth = beneath.depth + 1 }
                        if better cand here then
                            { state = map.insert edge.from cand acc.state, changed = True }
                        else acc)
            { state = state, changed = False }
            edges

    // Relaxation rather than a traversal, because a corpus graph may hold cycles and a recursive
    // walk down the links would not come back. The budget is the backstop, not the argument.
    //
    // The `in` is not optional, and its absence is not a type error. Without it gluon's layout
    // draws the next `let` into the recursive group, and its compiler then panics on a binding it
    // cannot see a record in — an internal error naming `final`, from inside gluon. Every other
    // binding here runs on layout alone; this one is written out because the failure it prevents
    // is one no amount of reading this file explains.
    //
    // Since #1106 that panic is contained: the run refuses this capability by name and exits
    // non-zero rather than aborting, and the refusal says the panic was gluon's. It is still a
    // failure with nothing useful in it, so the `in` stays.
    rec let settle state =
        let round = relax state
        if round.changed then settle round.state else round.state
    in

    let final = settle start

    // The fallback is unreachable — `settle` only ever replaces entries `start` put in, and
    // `start` covers every node. It is written out rather than left as a `map.find` unwrap
    // because there is no unwrap to reach for here: `Option` is a sum, and the second arm is what
    // makes this total. It is also the answer that would be right if it ever did fire.
    let reading node =
        let alone : Reading = { tier = rank (declared node), witness = node.id, depth = 0 }
        match map.find node.id final with
        | Some s -> s
        | None -> alone

    let downgraded =
        foldable.foldl
            (\acc node -> if rank (declared node) > (reading node).tier then acc + 1 else acc)
            0
            corpus.nodes

    { signals =
        array.functor.map
            (\node ->
                let s = reading node
                { node = node.id,
                  values =
                      [{ name = "weakest_beneath", value = Text s.witness },
                       { name = "chain_depth", value = Int s.depth }] })
            corpus.nodes,
      summary =
          [{ name = "nodes", value = Int (array.len corpus.nodes) },
           { name = "downgraded", value = Int downgraded },
           { name = "links_to_non_nodes", value = Int links_to_non_nodes }] }
